#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4323eae-5ac4-58e8-b41d-627a420ef2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395} {:answer/id #uuid "d86a247c-1534-5a30-876a-7f6322464c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362396} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf6aae3-1138-5d90-8eb9-c165d7f56024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5f7754-33dd-530f-9750-dfe41fde0328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362398} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4ac6bc-381a-509a-b61f-97b7c35e4832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4323eae-5ac4-58e8-b41d-627a420ef2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa780bc-b03f-5573-8318-518efa9363ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90691e53-4061-50e3-bc0b-ef8fb637c9f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "Interpret the positive quantity calculated below as the magnitude of the rate of change in the direction of fastest decrease.\n\nAt a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSigned-rate note: $\\nabla f(2,-1)=(1,1)$ and the unit direction of fastest decrease is $\\mathbf u=(-1,-1)/\\sqrt2$. Therefore $D_{\\mathbf u}f(2,-1)=\\nabla f(2,-1)\\cdot\\mathbf u=-\\sqrt2$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt2$, which is the positive quantity requested in the clarified study question."}]}
